[2026-03-15T01:02:31.309Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B1GhqXNQLGNhZd4WVdM68c","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (66.9KB). Full output saved to: /home/agent/.claude/projects/-repo/6b915d7b-b9f5-4445-990b-d343040d58c6/tool-results/toolu_01B1GhqXNQLGNhZd4WVdM68c.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n     1→     1→# Skill\n     2→     2→\n     3→     3→You will run all the tests in the app and get them to pass..\n     4→     4→\n     5→     5→## Unpack Subtasks\n     6→     6→\n     7→     7→List all test files in the app's `tests/` directory. Add ALL test file tasks in a single\n     8→     8→`add-task` call, one task per test file in alphabetical order. Write out every task\n     9→     9→explicitly — do not use a loop or script:\n    10→    10→\n    11→    11→```bash\n    12→    12→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    13→    13→[\n    14→    14→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file1>.spec.ts\"] },\n    15→    15→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file2>.spec.ts\"] }\n    16→    16→]\n    17→    17→EOF\n    18→    18→```\n    19→    19→\n    20→    20→After running tests and there are failures, pick specific failing tests and add a task to fix\n    21→    21→them without regressing any tests that passed in previous runs:\n    22→    22→\n    23→    23→```bash\n    24→    24→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    25→    25→[{ \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"Fix: <failing test name 1>\", \"Fix: <failing test name 2>\"] }]\n    26→    26→EOF\n    27→    27→```\n    28→    28→\n    29→    29→## Requirements\n    30→    30→\n    31→    31→Once all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\n    32→    32→When getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\n    33→    33→You must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\n    34→    34→disabling test functionality, and so on.\n    35→    35→\n    36→    36→You might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\n    37→    37→that the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\n    38→    38→entry in tests.md\n    39→    39→\n...\n</persisted-output>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6b915d7b-b9f5-4445-990b-d343040d58c6","uuid":"da3f5385-23d4-4312-8352-1f01271f81e1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agent/.claude/projects/-repo/6b915d7b-b9f5-4445-990b-d343040d58c6/tool-results/toolu_01YLkrLRzCkCM46UjrHrT2sr.txt","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→You will run all the tests in the app and get them to pass..\n     4→\n     5→## Unpack Subtasks\n     6→\n     7→List all test files in the app's `tests/` directory. Add ALL test file tasks in a single\n     8→`add-task` call, one task per test file in alphabetical order. Write out every task\n     9→explicitly — do not use a loop or script:\n    10→\n    11→```bash\n    12→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    13→[\n    14→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file1>.spec.ts\"] },\n    15→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"FixTests: tests/<file2>.spec.ts\"] }\n    16→]\n    17→EOF\n    18→```\n    19→\n    20→After running tests and there are failures, pick specific failing tests and add a task to fix\n    21→them without regressing any tests that passed in previous runs:\n    22→\n    23→```bash\n    24→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    25→[{ \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"Fix: <failing test name 1>\", \"Fix: <failing test name 2>\"] }]\n    26→EOF\n    27→```\n    28→\n    29→## Requirements\n    30→\n    31→Once all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\n    32→When getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\n    33→You must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\n    34→disabling test functionality, and so on.\n    35→\n    36→You might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\n    37→that the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\n    38→entry in tests.md\n    39→\n    40→You MUST read these skill files before testing.\n    41→\n    42→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md\n    43→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n    44→\n    45→RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY is already set in the environment for using the Replay CLI.\n    46→\n    47→## Replay.io Playwright Installation and Configuration\n    48→\n    49→Every app MUST use the Replay browser to record Playwright test runs. This section describes\n    50→the exact installation steps and configuration required.\n    51→\n    52→### 1. Install dependencies\n    53→\n    54→The app's `package.json` must include these dev dependencies:\n    55→\n    56→```bash\n    57→npm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright @playwright/test\n    58→```\n    59→\n    60→`@replayio/playwright` (currently v5.0.1) provides the `replayReporter` and `devices` exports\n    61→for integrating Replay recordings into Playwright.\n    62→\n    63→### 2. Install the Replay browser\n    64→\n    65→Run once in the container (or as part of CI setup):\n    66→\n    67→```bash\n    68→npx replayio install\n    69→```\n    70→\n    71→This downloads the Replay Chromium browser to `~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome`.\n    72→Verify it exists:\n    73→\n    74→```bash\n    75→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n    76→```\n    77→\n    78→### 3. API key\n    79→\n    80→`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\n    81→container. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n    82→\n    83→```ts\n    84→apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n    85→```\n    86→\n    87→### 4. Playwright config\n    88→\n    89→The Playwright config MUST:\n    90→\n    91→1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n    92→2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n    93→   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n    94→3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n    95→   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n    96→   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n    97→   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n    98→   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n    99→\n   100→**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n   101→`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\n   102→which breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n   103→\n   104→**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\n   105→operations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n   106→\n   107→- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n   108→- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n   109→- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n   110→\n   111→Example:\n   112→\n   113→```ts\n   114→import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\n   115→import { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n   116→\n   117→export default defineConfig({\n   118→  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n   119→  actionTimeout: 15000,\n   120→  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n   121→  reporter: [\n   122→    replayReporter({\n   123→      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n   124→      upload: false,\n   125→    }),\n   126→    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n   127→    ['list'],\n   128→  ],\n   129→  use: {\n   130→    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n   131→  },\n   132→});\n   133→```\n   134→\n   135→### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n   136→\n   137→- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n   138→- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n   139→  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n   140→  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n   141→- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n   142→- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n   143→- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n   144→  data accumulating).\n   145→\n   146→### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n   147→\n   148→The Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\n   149→container only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\n   150→test output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n   151→`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\n   152→the deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n   153→\n   154→### 7. Pre-test verification\n   155→\n   156→Before running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n   157→\n   158→```bash\n   159→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n   160→```\n   161→\n   162→If it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\n   163→without the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\n   164→Do not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n   165→\n   166→## Pre-Flight Checks\n   167→\n   168→Before running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\n   169→Run each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\n   170→single command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\n   171→process exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n   172→\n   173→```bash\n   174→# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\n   175→pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n   176→# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n   177→# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\n   178→fuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n   179→```\n   180→```bash\n   181→# Step 2: Verify env vars\n   182→grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env || echo \"ERROR: NEON_PROJECT_ID not set\"\n   183→```\n   184→```bash\n   185→# Step 3: Verify Replay browser\n   186→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome 2>/dev/null || npx replayio install\n   187→```\n   188→```bash\n   189→# Step 4: Verify dependencies\n   190→ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install --legacy-peer-deps\n   191→```\n   192→```bash\n   193→# Step 5: Clear stale recordings\n   194→npx replayio remove --all 2>/dev/null\n   195→```\n   196→\n   197→See `skills/scripts/preflight.md` for the full pre-flight checklist and\n   198→`skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for environment prerequisites.\n   199→\n   200→## Running Tests\n   201→\n   202→Run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\n   203→for the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n   204→\n   205→ULTRA IMPORTANT: NEVER EVER EVER RUN `npx playwright test`. You must use the provided test script.\n   206→\n   207→- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n   208→- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n   209→- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n   210→- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n   211→\n   212→**ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED on first run**: If the first test run fails with\n   213→`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the dev server likely wasn't fully ready. Wait 5 seconds and retry\n   214→once before investigating further. Do not immediately launch into manual `netlify dev` startup\n   215→or port debugging — the test script manages the server automatically and a single retry usually\n   216→resolves transient startup delays.\n   217→\n   218→**Retry limit**: Limit test re-runs to 3 per failing test before investigating the root cause.\n   219→Test failures during development are expected (test-fix-retest cycle), but re-running the same\n   220→test more than 3 times without changing approach indicates the fix strategy is wrong. Stop,\n   221→analyze the failure more carefully (use Replay if available), and try a different approach.\n   222→\n   223→**Batch failure triage**: When multiple tests fail, categorize failures before fixing them\n   224→one-by-one. Common categories (data contamination, missing serial blocks, non-unique test data)\n   225→often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\n   226→tests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n   227→\n   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→\n   230→**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n   231→(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\n   232→API response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\n   233→In observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n   234→\n   235→Use Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\n   236→values or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\n   237→Specifically:\n   238→- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n   239→  output is always sufficient.\n   240→- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n   241→- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n   242→- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n   243→- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n   244→- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n   245→\n   246→In observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\n   247→were unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\n   248→failures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\n   249→responses without visual debugging.\n   250→\n   251→## Debugging\n   252→\n   253→When tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\n   254→mandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n   255→\n   256→0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   257→   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   258→   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   259→   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n   260→1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n   261→2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   262→   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   263→   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   264→   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   265→   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   266→   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   267→   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   268→   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   269→   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n   270→3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   271→   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   272→   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   273→   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   274→   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   275→   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n   276→4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n   277→5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   278→   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   279→   making any changes.\n   280→6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   281→   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n   282→7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   283→   on what you found.\n   284→8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   285→   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   286→   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n   287→\n   288→### Bug Writeup Template\n   289→\n   290→For each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n   291→\n   292→```\n   293→# Bug: <test name>\n   294→\n   295→## Step 1: Evidence\n   296→\n   297→Evidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\n   298→responses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\n   299→no evidence.>\n   300→\n   301→Evidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\n   302→selectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n   303→\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n   304→\n   305→## Step 2: Determination\n   306→\n   307→Which is broken: <APP or TEST>\n   308→\n   309→## Step 3: Root Cause\n   310→\n   311→<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\n   312→function(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n   313→```\n   314→\n   315→Do NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\n   316→Do NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\n   317→Do NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\n   318→The Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n   319→\n   320→When testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n   321→\n   322→## Test Isolation Mandates\n   323→\n   324→These rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\n   325→failures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n   326→\n   327→1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   328→   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   329→   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   330→   failures. The pattern is:\n   331→   ```typescript\n   332→   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n   333→     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n   334→     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n   335→     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   336→   });\n   337→   ```\n   338→\n   339→2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   340→   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   341→   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n   342→\n   343→3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   344→   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   345→   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   346→   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   347→   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   348→   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   349→   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   350→   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n   351→\n   352→4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   353→   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   354→   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   355→   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   356→   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n   357→\n   358→5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   359→   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   360→   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n   361→\n   362→6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   363→   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n   364→\n   365→7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   366→   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   367→   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   368→   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   369→   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n   370→\n   371→8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   372→   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   373→   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   374→   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   375→   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   376→   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   377→   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n   378→\n   379→   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   380→   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   381→   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   382→   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   383→   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n   384→\n   385→9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   386→   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   387→   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n   388→\n   389→10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   390→   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   391→   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   392→   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n   393→\n   394→11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n   395→    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n   396→    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n   397→    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n   398→    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n   399→    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n   400→\n   401→12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   402→   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   403→   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   404→   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   405→   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n   406→\n   407→12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   408→   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   409→   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   410→   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n   411→\n   412→13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n   413→    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n   414→    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n   415→    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n   416→\n   417→14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   418→   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   419→   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   420→   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   421→   overcounting.\n   422→\n   423→14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n   424→    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n   425→    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n   426→    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n   427→    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n   428→    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n   429→\n   430→15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n   431→    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n   432→    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n   433→    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n   434→\n   435→16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n   436→    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n   437→    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n   438→    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n   439→    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n   440→    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n   441→    duplication.\n   442→\n   443→17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n   444→    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n   445→    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n   446→    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n   447→    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n   448→    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n   449→    was overridden by a later worker.\n   450→\n   451→18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n   452→    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n   453→    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n   454→    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n   455→    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n   456→    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n   457→\n   458→19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n   459→    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n   460→    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n   461→    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n   462→    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n   463→\n   464→## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n   465→\n   466→Before committing a new spec file, verify:\n   467→- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n   468→- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n   469→- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n   470→- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n   471→- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n   472→\n   473→## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n   474→\n   475→When running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\n   476→must have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\n   477→causes cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\n   478→state. Isolation strategies:\n   479→\n   480→1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n   481→2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   482→   re-seed) between each spec file.\n   483→3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   484→   of batch contamination.\n   485→4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   486→   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   487→   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   488→   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n   489→5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs\n   490→   from API responses rather than hardcoding UUIDs. Hardcoded UUIDs that exist in one database\n   491→   state may not exist in the deployed database, causing seed-data-mismatch failures. Always\n   492→   query the API for the list of entities and select by name or attribute, not by UUID.\n   493→6. **JourneyQA must default to deployed URL**: JourneyQA tests should always target the\n   494→   deployed URL, not the local dev server. Local dev server tests consistently fail with\n   495→   Replay's Chromium browser due to browser/server incompatibility. Avoid the local server\n   496→   entirely for JourneyQA tasks.\n   497→\n   498→## React StrictMode and Double-Render\n   499→\n   500→React StrictMode (enabled by default in development) intentionally double-invokes render\n   501→functions, effects, and callbacks to help detect side effects. This causes:\n   502→\n   503→- **Double API calls**: Effects that fetch data will fire twice. Tests that count exact API\n   504→  calls (e.g., expecting exactly 1 fetch) will see 2. Use `waitFor` patterns that assert on\n   505→  DOM state rather than counting network requests.\n   506→- **Race conditions**: Two concurrent fetches for the same data can cause state flicker or\n   507→  stale data display. Components should handle this with cleanup functions in useEffect\n   508→  (abort controllers) or by ignoring stale responses.\n   509→- **Test flakiness**: If tests assert on intermediate render states, StrictMode's extra\n   510→  render cycle can cause intermittent failures. Always wait for the final stable state\n   511→  using `expect(...).toBeVisible()` or `expect(...).toPass()` rather than asserting\n   512→  immediately after navigation.\n   513→\n   514→When writing tests, assume double-renders will occur and avoid assertions that depend on\n   515→exact render or fetch counts. Use DOM-based assertions (`toBeVisible`, `toHaveText`) that\n   516→naturally wait for the final state.\n   517→\n   518→## useEffect Race Conditions\n   519→\n   520→A common pattern in app bugs (observed in multiple test sessions) is `useEffect` hooks that\n   521→overwrite user input with stale API data. This occurs when:\n   522→\n   523→1. User starts editing a form field (local state changes)\n   524→2. An API response arrives (from a fetch triggered by a prior render or StrictMode double-mount)\n   525→3. The `useEffect` that processes the API response overwrites local state, discarding user input\n   526→\n   527→**The fix pattern**: Add an \"editing guard\" that prevents API data from overwriting local state\n   528→while the user is actively editing. Two common implementations:\n   529→\n   530→```typescript\n   531→// Pattern 1: useRef editing flag\n   532→const isEditingRef = useRef(false);\n   533→\n   534→useEffect(() => {\n   535→  if (!isEditingRef.current && apiData) {\n   536→    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n   537→  }\n   538→}, [apiData]);\n   539→\n   540→// Set isEditingRef.current = true in onChange, false in onBlur/onSave\n   541→```\n   542→\n   543→```typescript\n   544→// Pattern 2: pendingSavesRef to track in-flight saves\n   545→const pendingSavesRef = useRef(0);\n   546→\n   547→const handleSave = async () => {\n   548→  pendingSavesRef.current++;\n   549→  await saveToApi(value);\n   550→  pendingSavesRef.current--;\n   551→};\n   552→\n   553→useEffect(() => {\n   554→  if (pendingSavesRef.current === 0 && apiData) {\n   555→    setLocalValue(apiData.value);\n   556→  }\n   557→}, [apiData]);\n   558→```\n   559→\n   560→When debugging tests where user input appears to be \"lost\" or fields revert to old values,\n   561→check for unguarded `useEffect` hooks that sync API data to local state. This pattern is\n   562→most reliably diagnosed using Replay recordings to observe the exact sequence of state updates.\n   563→\n   564→## Test Counting\n   565→\n   566→Do NOT use `npx playwright test --list` to count or list tests — it is unreliable and\n   567→frequently fails or produces unhelpful output (~20% success rate). Instead, use:\n   568→\n   569→```bash\n   570→grep -c \"test('\" <file>\n   571→```\n   572→\n   573→This reliably counts the number of tests in a spec file for verifying the per-file test limit.\n   574→\n   575→## Test Command Reliability\n   576→\n   577→`npm run test` has a ~59% clean-pass rate (41% of runs encounter at least one failure).\n   578→Test failures during development are expected — they are part of the test-fix-retest cycle.\n   579→Do not treat a first-run failure as a sign of a fundamental problem. Follow the debugging\n   580→process in the section below, and respect the 3-retry limit before changing approach.\n   581→\n   582→## Directives\n   583→\n   584→- Do NOT manually start `netlify dev` for testing. The test script manages the dev server\n   585→  automatically. Only use manual `netlify dev` for one-off curl checks.\n   586→\n   587→- If you do manually start `netlify dev` for curl testing, you MUST pass `--offline` and\n   588→  `--functions ./netlify/functions`. The `--offline` flag is required in this container\n   589→  environment to avoid authentication failures. The `--functions` flag avoids 404 errors on\n   590→  function endpoints (the `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions directory to\n   591→  resolve incorrectly without it). Example:\n   592→  `npx netlify dev --offline --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n   593→\n   594→- The Playwright `webServer` command should also include `--functions ./netlify/functions` when\n   595→  using Netlify Functions, to ensure function endpoints resolve correctly during test runs.\n   596→  Example: `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n   597→\n   598→- After making code changes to Netlify Functions or frontend code during a testing session, ALWAYS\n   599→  restart the dev server before re-running tests. The dev server may cache old function bundles.\n   600→  Kill background processes with `pkill -f \"netlify\"` and `pkill -f \"vite\"` before restarting.\n   601→\n   602→- When a checkDirectives finding suggests removing test code (e.g., \"redundant\" cleanup,\n   603→  \"unnecessary\" helpers), always run the affected tests *before* committing the removal to\n   604→  confirm the change is safe. Cleanup code that looks redundant may be essential for test\n   605→  isolation.\n   606→\n   607→- **Require `data-testid` for all assertable elements.** Tests MUST NOT rely on CSS selectors\n   608→  tied to colors, styles, or computed attributes (e.g., `path[stroke=\"#4d7c5e\"]`,\n   609→  `[style*=\"background: red\"]`). Always use `data-testid` attributes for test selectors.\n   610→  This prevents self-inflicted failures when refactoring tasks (e.g., checkDirectives)\n   611→  replace hardcoded colors with CSS variables.\n   612→\n   613→- **Selector audit step for refactoring tasks.** When checkDirectives removes hardcoded\n   614→  colors or replaces them with CSS variables, the task MUST grep test files for selectors\n   615→  that depend on the affected values before committing (e.g.,\n   616→  `grep -r \"stroke=\\|fill=\\|background:\" tests/`). Update any matching test selectors to\n   617→  use `data-testid` in the same changeset.\n   618→\n   619→- **Use content-based locators over positional.** Tests should use `filter({ hasText })` or\n   620→  `getByText()` instead of `nth()` for dropdown/list assertions to avoid ordering-dependent\n   621→  failures. API response ordering is not guaranteed to match creation order.\n   622→\n   623→- **Use exact text matching for option selection.** When selecting dropdown options or matching\n   624→  text that contains common words (e.g., \"Monthly\", \"Active\"), always use exact matching to\n   625→  avoid strict-mode violations from substring collisions. Use `{ exact: true }` or regex\n   626→  anchors: `getByText('Monthly', { exact: true })` or `hasText: /^Monthly$/`. Without exact\n   627→  matching, \"Monthly\" matches both \"Monthly\" and \"Bi-Monthly\", causing Playwright strict-mode\n   628→  errors.\n   629→\n   630→- **Avoid `.or()` locator patterns.** Playwright's `.or()` combinator creates a locator matching\n   631→  elements from either branch. When both branches resolve to elements on the page, the combined\n   632→  locator matches multiple elements and triggers strict-mode violations. Use a single specific\n   633→  locator (`getByTestId` or `getByRole` with exact matching) instead of `.or()` fallback chains.\n   634→\n   635→- All browsers must run headless. Never use Xvfb, never set `DISPLAY`, never use the `replayio record`\n   636→  CLI (it launches a headed browser). Use `@replayio/playwright` for recordings.\n   637→\n   638→- Do NOT wrap test commands with `timeout` or `nohup`. Neither works reliably in this\n   639→  environment — `timeout` exits with signal errors (exit code 124/137) and `nohup` fails\n   640→  to background processes correctly. Let the test script manage its own lifecycle.\n   641→\n   642→- Tests run serially with `--workers 1`. The test script resets the database between each test.\n   643→\n   644→- Tests MUST NOT run against the production database or the main Neon branch. The test script\n   645→  creates ephemeral Neon branches for test runs and deletes them afterwards.\n   646→\n   647→- NEVER stop, cancel, or skip a Replay recording upload that is in progress. The upload is a\n   648→  prerequisite for Replay MCP analysis, which is mandatory for debugging failures. If the upload\n   649→  is taking a long time, WAIT for it to finish. Do NOT proceed with fixes based on \"enough\n   650→  information\" — you must complete the full upload → MCP analysis → fix cycle. Stopping an\n   651→  upload to \"save time\" violates the debugging process and leads to guesswork-based fixes.\n   652→\n   653→- Always run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` (from the app directory), never by calling\n   654→  `npx playwright test` directly. The test script includes essential pre-test setup (branch\n   655→  creation, schema init, seeding, stale cleanup) that is skipped when running Playwright directly.\n   656→\n   657→## Tips\n   658→\n   659→- When a test times out inside a `.toPass()` block, use `mcp__replay__PlaywrightSteps` first. It\n   660→  shows the exact sequence of Playwright actions with timestamps and return values. Look for a\n   661→  step that is stuck auto-waiting for an element — this reveals nested-wait deadlocks where the\n   662→  DOM changed mid-iteration and an inner `textContent()` or `count()` call is waiting on an\n   663→  element that was removed. The fix is always in the test: replace the iteration loop with a\n   664→  single atomic Playwright assertion (see the nested-wait directive in writeTests.md).\n   665→- When debugging history/timeline tests, check for duplicate entries caused by React re-renders triggering multiple API calls, and check for missing entries from mutation handlers that skip history writes.\n   666→- When many detail-page tests fail with \"expected count > 0, received 0\", the root cause is almost\n   667→  always that the database has no seed data OR the API functions are not working. Check both before\n   668→  attempting to fix individual tests.\n   669→- When filter/search tests return wrong data (e.g., filtering by \"proposal\" returns \"Qualification\"),\n   670→  the root cause may be that the SQL query is silently broken. Verify the API returns correctly\n   671→  filtered results by curling the endpoint directly with filter parameters.\n   672→- Tests that pass individually but fail in the full suite usually indicate data contamination between\n   673→  tests. The test script resets the database between each test, so contamination should not occur.\n   674→  If it does, check that the reset is working correctly.\n   675→- When test IDs differ between spec files (cross-cutting vs page-specific), decide on ONE canonical\n   676→  set of IDs in the component and update whichever test file has fewer references.\n   677→- When testing unauthenticated scenarios with `browser.newContext()`, always pass\n   678→  `storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] }` to ensure a truly empty context. Without this,\n   679→  Supabase or other auth libraries may detect cached sessions from previous test runs, causing\n   680→  \"unauthenticated\" tests to appear authenticated.\n   681→- If many tests suddenly fail with `net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED` or `ERR_CONNECTION_RESET` partway\n   682→  through a test run, the dev server crashed mid-suite. Do NOT debug individual test failures —\n   683→  they are all caused by the same server crash. Re-run the full suite. If the crash recurs, check\n   684→  for memory pressure or port conflicts from zombie processes.\n   685→- Zombie processes (`<defunct>`) from previous `vite` or `netlify` runs are harmless and cannot be\n   686→  killed (they are orphaned child processes waiting for PID 1 to reap them). Ignore them. The\n   687→  Playwright `webServer` config starts fresh processes on different ports and is not affected.\n   688→- **Kill stale processes before every test run.** Port conflicts from stale `netlify dev`\n   689→  processes are a significant source of infrastructure failures (20 events affecting 225 test\n   690→  runs in one observed session). Always run the pre-flight cleanup (`pkill -f \"netlify|vite\"`,\n   691→  `fuser -k 8888/tcp`) before each test run, not just the first run in a session.\n   692→- Commit incrementally during debugging sessions. If you improve test results (e.g., 81→207 passing)\n   693→  or fix a meaningful issue, commit those changes immediately even if other failures remain. An\n   694→  iteration that produces zero commits despite significant work (skill updates, code fixes,\n   695→  debugging progress) is wasted effort because the next iteration starts from scratch.\n   696→- **Deployment tests (`tests/deployment.spec.ts`) only work after a deploy** and require a live\n   697→  production URL. Do NOT run deployment tests as part of regular FixTests workflows — they will\n   698→  return 0 tests or fail when run locally without a deployed environment. Only run them after\n   699→  `npm run deploy` has completed successfully.\n   700→- Before running deployment tests, verify that required environment variables (DATABASE_URL,\n   701→  etc.) are set on the deployment target (e.g., Netlify). Missing env vars cause infrastructure\n   702→  failures that waste a full test cycle. Use `netlify env:list` to check.\n   703→- Before running deployment tests, also verify that all Netlify Functions are actually deployed\n   704→  by curling at least one API endpoint. Functions may silently fail to deploy if the `--functions`\n   705→  flag was missing or if there were build errors. A quick `curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\"\n   706→  https://<site-url>/api/<function>` returning 200 confirms functions are live.\n   707→- Before running tests, verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set in the environment. The test script\n   708→  requires it for creating ephemeral Neon branches.\n   709→- Running the full test suite at once (e.g., `npx playwright test` with no file argument) can\n   710→  OOM or crash with `ERR_STRING_TOO_LONG` on large suites. Always run tests one spec file at a\n   711→  time using `npm run test tests/<file>.spec.ts` (which uses the test script that manages\n   712→  database branches). When verifying broad changes, pick the most relevant 2-3 spec files.\n   713→- The database is reset between each test, so tests do not need to worry about data from\n   714→  previous tests. Each test starts with a fresh seed.\n   715→- Seed scripts must always call `truncateAllTables()` (or equivalent) before inserting data\n   716→  to ensure idempotency. Without truncation, re-running seeds causes duplicate key errors.\n   717→  Every test file's `beforeAll`/`beforeEach` should TRUNCATE relevant tables before inserting\n   718→  seed data, not just delete known IDs. Hardcoded ID deletion breaks when Neon branches\n   719→  inherit data from parent branches. **Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()`\n   720→  alone** — Neon ephemeral branches inherit parent data, so insert-only seeding produces\n   721→  duplicates.\n   722→- The Vite dev server cold start can cause the first test in a suite to timeout. Consider adding\n   723→  a warm-up navigation in `beforeAll` or increasing the first test's timeout to account for\n   724→  cold start latency.\n   725→- Avoid hardcoding expected values in assertions. Tests that hardcode specific counts (e.g.,\n   726→  `expect 1 remaining task`) or specific names (e.g., `\"David Lee\"`) break when test data\n   727→  changes. Use relative assertions or query actual seed data to derive expected values.\n   728→  Tests that verify \"a new item was added\" must count items before and after the action,\n   729→  not assert a hardcoded total.\n   730→- **Wait for data before counting rows.** Any test that captures an initial count of list items\n   731→  (for add/delete assertions) MUST first wait for the first row to be visible:\n   732→  ```ts\n   733→  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').first()).toBeVisible();\n   734→  const initialCount = await page.locator('[data-testid=\"row\"]').count();\n   735→  ```\n   736→  Without this wait, async data loading may not have completed, returning 0 and causing\n   737→  off-by-one assertion failures. This is the single highest-impact testing pattern — it\n   738→  prevents ~38% of observed test failures.\n   739→- **Use edge coordinates for backdrop click tests.** Tests for modal dismissal via backdrop/overlay\n   740→  click should always use edge coordinates (e.g., `{ x: 10, y: 10 }`) rather than clicking the\n   741→  center of the overlay, as modals often occupy the center and intercept the click.\n   742→- **Validate seed data counts before asserting.** Tests that assert specific counts from seed data\n   743→  should reference `seed-db.ts` directly or query the actual count rather than hardcoding expected\n   744→  values. Seed data mismatches account for ~42% of observed test failures.\n   745→- Validate that seed data exists before asserting on it. If a test expects a specific assignee\n   746→  name or record count, verify the data is present first. This catches seed data mismatches\n   747→  early instead of producing confusing assertion failures.\n   748→- Use unique `data-testid` values across the page. When a page and a modal both contain similar\n   749→  elements (e.g., a technician select), prefix testids with the component context to avoid\n   750→  strict-mode violations (e.g., `modal-technician-select` vs `detail-technician-select`).\n   751→- Before writing tests that rely on `data-testid` attributes, verify those attributes exist in\n   752→  the component source. Missing `data-testid` attributes cause test failures that are easy to\n   753→  prevent with a quick source check.\n   754→- When seed data uses `NUMERIC` or `DECIMAL` database columns, expect formatted output in\n   755→  assertions (e.g., `\"500\"` not `\"500.00\"`). These column types return decimal strings from\n   756→  PostgreSQL, so the app must format them before display. If tests fail on numeric values,\n   757→  check the column type and app formatting layer before modifying test expectations.\n   758→- When navigation timeouts persist across retries for a specific app (e.g., all supplier-details\n   759→  pages timing out), this is likely a Replay browser resource issue rather than an app bug.\n   760→  Adding a retry-with-delay (e.g., 5s between retries) can help distinguish transient\n   761→  infrastructure issues from persistent ones. If retries still fail, skip those tests and\n   762→  note the infrastructure issue.\n   763→- Seed data that includes time values should use 24-hour format (e.g., `\"14:30\"` not\n   764→  `\"2:30 PM\"`) to avoid text-based sorting issues in SQL. When PostgreSQL sorts TIME or\n   765→  text-stored time values, 24-hour format produces correct chronological order while AM/PM\n   766→  format does not (e.g., `\"10:00 AM\"` sorts after `\"1:00 PM\"` alphabetically).\n   767→- Backend API functions should return date fields in a consistent format — either always\n   768→  ISO timestamps (`2026-01-15T00:00:00.000Z`) or always `YYYY-MM-DD` strings, not a mix\n   769→  of both. Mixed formats cause frontend parsing issues when components expect one format\n   770→  but receive the other.\n   771→- **Format currency values with `.toFixed(2)`.** All currency display components must format\n   772→  values with 2 decimal places (e.g., `175.00` not `175`). Multiple test failures arise from\n   773→  assertions expecting formatted currency strings. Apply `.toFixed(2)` or equivalent formatting\n   774→  at the display layer for any monetary amount.\n   775→- **Use `type=\"text\" inputMode=\"decimal\"` for currency inputs from the start.** Avoid\n   776→  `<input type=\"number\">` for currency fields — it strips formatting and causes issues with\n   777→  decimal display. Using `type=\"text\"` with `inputMode=\"decimal\"` provides the numeric\n   778→  keyboard on mobile while allowing full control over formatting. Converting from `type=\"number\"`\n   779→  to `type=\"text\"` mid-stream breaks existing test expectations.\n   780→- **Seed data MUST use relative date offsets from `new Date()`** rather than hardcoded date\n   781→  strings or month names. Tests that assert on date-filtered data (e.g., expecting \"Jan\"\n   782→  entries or \"No upcoming visits\") will fail when run on different days or months. Use\n   783→  expressions like `new Date(Date.now() + 86400000)` for \"tomorrow\" or subtract days for\n   784→  past dates. This prevents failures when tests run on different days than when seeds were\n   785→  written — hardcoded dates caused a cluster of 3 test failures in one observed session.\n   786→- **Verify directories before navigating.** Before using `cd` to navigate to an app directory,\n   787→  verify it exists with `test -d` or `ls`. Directory navigation failures (`cd` to nonexistent\n   788→  paths) are the most common command failure across all worker iterations.\n   789→- Avoid redundant file exploration commands (`ls /repo/apps/`, `find ... -type f`, etc.)\n   790→  across test runs. Once you know the project structure, do not re-discover it in every\n   791→  iteration. Use the Glob and Grep tools instead of shell commands for file operations.\n   792→\n   793→- **Verify seed data covers all test tables.** After writing or modifying seed-database\n   794→  functions, verify that all tables referenced in tests are properly seeded. Missing table\n   795→  seeding (e.g., categories table truncated but never re-seeded) causes tests to time out\n   796→  waiting for data that doesn't exist. Check each `TRUNCATE` call has a corresponding `INSERT`.\n   797→\n   798→- **Validate Netlify function URL routing.** After writing backend functions, verify that\n   799→  `[[redirects]]` in `netlify.toml` includes all `/api/*` patterns and that URL segment\n   800→  parsing uses the correct index. A recurring bug is `segments[N]` off-by-one errors where\n   801→  the function reads `undefined` instead of the resource ID, returning 405. This appeared in\n   802→  3 separate functions in one session.\n   803→\n   804→- **Cross-spec learning**: When a fix pattern is discovered in one spec file (e.g.,\n   805→  wait-before-count, destructive test reordering, formatDate normalization), proactively apply\n   806→  it to all other spec files in the same app before re-running tests. Fixing each file\n   807→  independently wastes re-run cycles on the same known issue.\n   808→- **Validate journey/QA test prerequisites.** Before running journey or QA tests that depend on\n   809→  specific data states (e.g., member with points > 0, active subscriptions), verify the test data\n   810→  meets preconditions. A test that selects a member with 0 points to test redemption will fail at\n   811→  the UI level (disabled button) rather than giving a clear data error. Add precondition checks\n   812→  or use seed data that guarantees the required state.\n   813→- When many tests are pre-existing failures unrelated to the current task, avoid re-verifying\n   814→  them on every run. Use the `git stash` triage approach (see `skills/debugging/README.md`)\n   815→  once per task to confirm, then focus on new failures only.\n   816→- Before writing test assertions on data counts or relationships, verify expectations against\n   817→  actual seed data. 30% of observed failures were seed-data-mismatch — tests assumed wrong\n   818→  counts or relationships that didn't exist in the database.\n   819→- When building forms with custom validation, always add `noValidate` to the `<form>` element\n   820→  to prevent native browser validation from blocking custom logic. Without `noValidate`,\n   821→  `<input type=\"number\">` and `<input type=\"email\">` have built-in validation that fires\n   822→  before the form's `onSubmit` handler, causing custom validation tests to fail silently.\n   823→- Establish a consistent date handling convention: components should always format dates from\n   824→  ISO timestamps (what the API returns), and date inputs should always use `YYYY-MM-DD`\n   825→  format. Mismatches between API response format and component expectations are a recurring\n   826→  source of failures. All date comparisons in frontend code and tests must normalize to\n   827→  `YYYY-MM-DD` format before comparing, using `.split('T')[0]` or equivalent, since Postgres\n   828→  TIMESTAMP/DATE columns may return ISO strings with time components.\n   829→- When filtering by status values like \"Active\"/\"Inactive\", use exact text matching\n   830→  (e.g., `getByRole('option', { name: /^Active$/ })`) to avoid Playwright strict-mode\n   831→  violations from substring collisions (e.g., \"Active\" matching both \"Active\" and \"Inactive\").\n   832→- Tests should never use raw HTML element selectors (`tr`, `td`, `li`) to locate rows or items.\n   833→  Always use `data-testid` attributes instead. Raw element selectors break when the component's\n   834→  HTML structure changes (e.g., switching from `<table>` to `<div>`-based layout), causing\n   835→  timeouts that are hard to diagnose.\n   836→- **Click visible labels for custom checkboxes/toggles.** When testing checkboxes or toggle\n   837→  inputs with custom CSS styling, the native `<input>` may be hidden and overlaid by a styled\n   838→  element (e.g., `<span>`, `<label>`). Playwright's `click()` on the hidden input will fail\n   839→  with \"click intercepted by...\" errors. Always click the visible label or wrapper element\n   840→  instead of the hidden input. Check the component source to identify which element is visually\n   841→  clickable.\n   842→\n   843→- **Document seed data in test specs.** Test spec files should include a comment at the top\n   844→  documenting what seed data exists (e.g., number of pre-existing records, their names/types)\n   845→  so test authors can account for it upfront rather than discovering mismatches at runtime.\n   846→\n   847→- **Use `click()` + `type()` instead of `fill()` for onChange-dependent inputs.** Playwright's\n   848→  `fill()` sets the input value directly and only fires `input` and `change` events at the end,\n   849→  which may not trigger React's synthetic `onChange` handler in all component implementations.\n   850→  If `fill()` doesn't trigger the expected behavior, switch to `click()` on the input followed\n   851→  by `type()` to simulate real keystrokes, which fires `onChange` on each character.\n   852→\n   853→- **Trigger onBlur after fill() for blur-persisted forms.** When testing form components that\n   854→  use `onBlur` for persistence (auto-save on blur), Playwright `fill()` alone won't trigger\n   855→  the save. Always add `.blur()` after `.fill()` calls in these cases:\n   856→  ```ts\n   857→  await page.locator('#field').fill('value');\n   858→  await page.locator('#field').blur();\n   859→  ```\n   860→\n   861→- **Use ESM-compatible __dirname.** If test helpers or server code use `__dirname`, ensure\n   862→  ESM-compatible alternatives are used: `import.meta.dirname` (Node 21.2+) or\n   863→  `path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))`. CommonJS `__dirname` is not available in\n   864→  ESM modules and will crash at runtime.\n   865→\n   866→- **Add useEffect editing guards for editable forms.** Any React form that loads data via\n   867→  useEffect and allows editing must include an `isEditing` state guard that prevents useEffect\n   868→  from overwriting user input during editing. Set `isEditing = true` when the user begins\n   869→  editing, and skip the data-loading useEffect when `isEditing` is true. This pattern was\n   870→  needed across 3 spec files (7 tests) in one session — apply it proactively to all edit\n   871→  forms during initial component development.\n   872→- Ensure tests don't leak state between runs. Data-contamination failures occur when a prior\n   873→  test's API calls complete after the next test has started, polluting the data state. Use\n   874→  `test.describe.serial` for tests that share mutable state, or ensure API calls are fully\n   875→  settled before test completion.\n   876→- **Validate test data against DB CHECK constraints.** Before using enum values in test data\n   877→  (e.g., `visit_type`, `status`), verify them against the database schema's CHECK constraints\n   878→  or enum definitions. Invalid enum values cause constraint violations that surface as cryptic\n   879→  500 errors. This pattern appeared in 2+ spec files in observed sessions.\n   880→- Any test that creates records (orders, customers, etc.) should include a `beforeEach`\n   881→  cleanup helper (e.g., `deleteAllOrders()`) to prevent data accumulation across tests.\n   882→  Add this from the start when writing tests — don't wait for contamination failures.\n   883→- **Reset global settings in beforeEach.** Since settings are typically a single-row table,\n   884→  any spec that tests settings modifications (business name, preferences, etc.) should reset\n   885→  settings to known defaults in a `beforeEach` hook via API call. Otherwise, earlier tests\n   886→  that modify settings will contaminate later tests in the same file.\n   887→- Any test that deletes all records (e.g., empty state tests) must be in a\n   888→  `test.describe.serial` block at the end of the describe. Destructive tests placed earlier\n   889→  corrupt state for subsequent tests in the same file.\n   890→\n   891→- **Run targeted tests after accessibility refactors.** After any HTML structure change\n   892→  (element type changes like `<span>` to `<th>`, nesting changes), run the spec file for\n   893→  that component before committing. `npm run check` does not catch selector breakage — only\n   894→  running the actual tests will reveal it. This applies especially to polishApp accessibility\n   895→  refactors.\n   896→\n   897→- **JourneyQA tests must not assume live data state.** Do not assume records from prior test\n   898→  runs persist in the deployed database. Either (a) create all required seed data\n   899→  programmatically within the test, or (b) query the API at the start of the test to find an\n   900→  existing record rather than hard-coding an ID or name.\n   901→\n   902→- **Prefer `toContainText` for cells with potential sr-only text.** When asserting on table\n   903→  cells, score displays, or other elements that may contain visually-hidden accessibility\n   904→  annotations (e.g., sr-only \"(leader)\" spans), use `toContainText` instead of `toHaveText`.\n   905→  `toHaveText` requires an exact match including hidden text, which breaks when accessibility\n   906→  improvements add sr-only content. This pattern caused a cluster of 9 test failures across\n   907→  multiple spec files in one observed session — all resolved by switching to `toContainText`.\n   908→\n   909→- **Apply race-condition fixes across all slices at once.** When a race-condition fix (e.g.,\n   910→  request-id tracking) is applied to one Redux slice, check whether other slices using the\n   911→  same fetching pattern need the same fix, and apply it proactively. This avoids discovering\n   912→  the same bug independently in each slice across multiple test-fix cycles.\n   913→\n   914→- **Check test suite after cascade-delete fixes.** When fixing a backend DELETE endpoint to\n   915→  add cascade behavior (e.g., deleting dependent records before the parent), immediately check\n   916→  the test suite for any test that relies on the cascaded data existing later in the file.\n   917→  Fix both the backend and the test ordering in the same changeset — otherwise the cascade fix\n   918→  enables a destructive test to wipe data that subsequent tests depend on.\n   919→\n   920→- **Deploy before writing journey tests.** Journey tests that run against a deployed URL\n   921→  require the correct app to be deployed and the production DB seeded. Verify the deployed URL\n   922→  in `playwright.config.ts` before writing selectors. Do not write journey tests against a URL\n   923→  that belongs to a different app.\n   924→","numLines":924,"startLine":1,"totalLines":924}}}
